# Zeneva marketing recorder — credentials template.
#
# Copy this to the PROJECT ROOT as `.env.recorder`:
#
#   cp scripts/record/recorder.env.example .env.recorder
#
# `.env.recorder` is covered by the `.env*` line in .gitignore, so it stays out
# of the repo. This template is deliberately NOT named `.env.something` — that
# same ignore rule would swallow it and there would be nothing to copy from.
#
# Never put real values in this file, and never pass them as command-line flags:
# flags land in shell history and in `ps` output for every user on the machine.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- account

# Use a DEMO business, not your live one.
#
# Two reasons, both of which have teeth:
#   1. Whatever this account can see is what ends up in a public marketing video
#      — real customer names, real phone numbers, real revenue figures.
#   2. Every take is a real login and a real page load, so every take costs real
#      Firestore reads on an account you pay for.
#
# The recorder is read-only by default; `--commit` is what lets a flow actually
# ring up a sale or write a stock count, and it is off unless you ask for it.
ZENEVA_RECORD_EMAIL=demo@yourbusiness.com
ZENEVA_RECORD_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- target

# Which app to record. Local dev by default; point it at production only if you
# want production data in the footage.
#   http://localhost:9007      npm run dev
#   https://zeneva.space       live
ZENEVA_RECORD_URL=http://localhost:9007

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- audio

# Optional default music bed, so you don't retype --music on every take.
# A file, or a folder — a folder is searched for <flow>.mp3 (pos.mp3, zen.mp3,
# inventory.mp3) and falls back to the first track it finds.
#
# Use a track you actually have the rights to. These videos go out as marketing,
# which is exactly the case a content-ID claim is built to catch.
# ZENEVA_RECORD_MUSIC=D:/marketing/beds
